        "node_id": "n_1_42f418",
        "thought_text": "Consider the smallest monster's role: If the smallest monster is the last one standing, it will have the minimum health because it can always be the target of attacks without retaliating effectively.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794391163.632283
      },
      "rev_n_2_cb45e3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_cb45e3_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_cb45e3",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the parity (odd/even) of the health values: If all monsters have even health, the final health will be even. If there's an odd health value, the final health could be odd or even depending on the sequence of attacks.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794391163.6323252
      },
      "rev_n_3_3b1e0c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_3b1e0c_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_3b1e0c",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the process with a greedy approach: Always let the monster with the highest health attack the monster with the second-highest health until only one monster remains. This might not always yield the minimum but provides a structured way to estimate the outcome.",
